    "result_text": "All tests green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Change: household-capped membership level\n\nThe spec: a new owner's `membershipLevel` cannot exceed one above the current maximum `membershipLevel` among their household members; with no existing household member, no cap applies.\n\n`membershipLevel` was a value derived purely in `OwnerMapper` from `membershipPoints`, recomputed on every read. Since the cap depends on *other* household members (visible only at create time), I persist the capped value and have the mapper prefer it.\n\n**What I changed:**\n\n1. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a stored `membershipLevel` field (`membership_level` column) with getter/setter.\n\n2. **Schema files** (`db/{h2,hsqldb,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`) \u2014 added the `membership_level` column to the `owners` table.\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 split the level logic: `derivedMembershipLevel(owner)` keeps the points\u2192level derivation; `membershipLevel(owner)` now returns the stored (capped) value when present, else the derived value. Also ignored `membershipLevel` on `toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)`.\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 added `cappedMembershipLevel(owner, householdId)`: it takes the owner's derived level and, when active owners share the same `householdId`, caps it at `min(derived, maxExistingMemberLevel + 1)` (no existing member \u21d2 no cap). `addOwner` calls it after the level-affecting fields are populated and stores the result before saving.\n\n5. **Removed the hard `DuplicateHousehold` rejection** (call, now-unused private method, and its import). The acceptance test creates two owners in the same household (same last name + postcode) and requires both to be created so the cap can be exercised \u2014 this checkpoint replaces \"reject a second household member\" with \"admit it, but cap its membership level.\" Exact-identity duplicates are still blocked by `rejectDuplicateIdentity`, and the soft `possibleDuplicate` flagging still applies.\n\n**Result:** In the test, owner A (first, solo household) gets level 1; owner B (same household) derives level 2, capped to `min(2, 1+1)=2`, so `lb \u2264 la + 1` holds. `mvn test` \u2192 `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.",
